From 8a41fbcb1db9f721f94275098df084546e8738b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lars Schellhas van Kisfeld
 <lars.schellhas-van-kisfeld@schellhasconsulting.de>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 21:20:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: surface_battery: Add DPTF battery data

The battery subsystem of the Surface Aggregator Module EC provides the
data that platform power management (DPTF) uses: the battery's peak and
sustained power, its impedance and no-load voltage, the power available
from the connected sources and the rest-of-platform power. The EC sends
it as event 0x53 when the power source changes, and answers a request
with the same command ID. The driver already receives the event, but
drops it ("TODO: Implement support for DPTF event").

Request the data at probe time, when the external power changes and after
resume from suspend (it can be stale then, for example if the power was
disconnected while suspended), update it from the event, and expose it in
a "dptf" attribute group on the battery. The attribute names and units
follow the DPTF battery participant (drivers/acpi/dptf/dptf_power.c,
INT3532). The group name is neutral on purpose: the driver does not
implement that participant's interface.

An EC that does not support the command does not answer the request and
the request runs into a timeout, so the driver cannot use the request to
find out about the support. It handles the data only on devices that
have the "has_dptf" device property, which the Surface Aggregator
registry sets for the devices that support it. Everywhere else the
driver sends no request, ignores the event and does not create the
attribute group. If the request fails on a device with the property,
the driver logs a warning and goes on without the data.

The first byte of the data is a version, which is 1 on the Surface Book 3.
The driver warns once per device about any other value but still exposes
the data, since the layout is checked by its length.

User space uses this to reproduce the Windows power management, which
sets, for example, the platform power limit (psys) from the available
input power. On Linux it otherwise stays at its boot value.

Tested on a Surface Book 3 (15", i7-1065G7, 7.3.4-1-surface) with the
three patches built out of tree: the values for both batteries match
the raw EC data read through the SSAM debug device, and unplugging and
replugging the Surface PSU (with a USB-C charger connected) updated
the base battery's available input power within a second (119.25 W ->
65 W -> 119.25 W). Over 60 s at idle the driver sent no 0x53 request
and no SAM request timed out.

The analysis, the patch and this changelog were prepared with the help
of an AI coding assistant (Claude, Anthropic). I reviewed the change.

Link: https://github.com/linux-surface/surface-aggregator-module/issues/29
Link: https://github.com/linux-surface/linux-surface/issues/2383
Signed-off-by: Lars Schellhas van Kisfeld <lars.schellhas-van-kisfeld@schellhasconsulting.de>
Patchset: surface-sam
---
